# Days of Week — Memory Drill

## Purpose

Drill English day → Greek day name and reverse. Completely arbitrary, must be memorised. Used constantly for scheduling, plans, and past narrative ("last Monday", "on Thursday"). Also presented in /learn/essentials as reference; this is the timed production drill.

Implemented as 14 items in `days-of-week.tsx` — 7 days plus 7 time expressions — under two
categories, Days and Time expressions. Colour theme: olive. Returns to `/practice/blocks`.

Target: 3 seconds.

## The 7 forms

| English | Greek | Notes |
|---------|-------|-------|
| Monday | Δευτέρα (η) | feminine |
| Tuesday | Τρίτη (η) | feminine |
| Wednesday | Τετάρτη (η) | feminine |
| Thursday | Πέμπτη (η) | feminine |
| Friday | Παρασκευή (η) | feminine |
| Saturday | Σάββατο (το) | neuter |
| Sunday | Κυριακή (η) | feminine |

All feminine except Σάββατο (neuter). Worth noting — Saturday is the odd one out.

## In-context forms (teach alongside)

Days are used with `την` (accusative feminine) for "on [day]":
- on Monday = **τη Δευτέρα**
- on Saturday = **το Σάββατο** (neuter accusative)
- on Sunday = **την Κυριακή**

The in-context form is what learners actually produce in sentences. Consider drilling both: bare noun AND "on [day]" phrase.

## Time expressions (`category: "time"`) — implemented

| English | Greek |
|---------|-------|
| yesterday | χτες |
| today | σήμερα |
| tomorrow | αύριο |
| the day after tomorrow | μεθαύριο |
| last week | την περασμένη εβδομάδα |
| next week | την επόμενη εβδομάδα |
| the weekend | το Σαββατοκύριακο |

These 7 time adverbs are high-frequency and pure memorisation. They live as a second category in
this drill rather than a separate drill — the set is small enough that 14 items is one session.

The last three are seeded with their article/preposition intact (την περασμένη εβδομάδα, not
εβδομάδα) because that is the produced form; `matchPhonetic` also accepts the answer without the
leading article.

## Drill format

**Forward**: English → type Greek
**Reverse**: Greek → English, self-assess

Days are drilled as bare nouns. The in-context "on [day]" form (τη Δευτέρα / το Σάββατο) is **not**
implemented and would need a separate item set or a mode switch.

## Notes

- Saturday/Sunday distinction is a common source of errors — worth flagging if learner misses Σάββατο gender
- Accent positions matter: Δευτέρα/Τρίτη/Τετάρτη/Πέμπτη — all end in accented -α or -η
